WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:08.990
- My name is Dan Powell.

00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:12.910
I am the Director of Community
Platforms here in Central IT.

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:15.070
Presenting with me today, I
have two of my colleagues,

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:17.173
James Tucciarone and Mike van Emmenes.

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:19.710
And we're gonna talk to you a little bit

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:22.010
about the technologies that we offer

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:24.640
that you can use to engage
with your community.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.660
How many of you were able to
see Nate Nickerson this morning

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.880
and talking about audience insights?

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:32.484
Very cool, so, a lot of the technologies

00:00:32.484 --> 00:00:34.323
that I'm gonna be talking about,

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.230
the data of which will give you insight

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:40.520
into what your community
experience actually is,

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:42.730
and once you have that data
then you can turn around,

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:45.730
you can make better decisions
about the experience

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:47.820
that you want somebody to
have with your department

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.033
or your school or unit,

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:55.160
and you can start to create
that experience for them

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:56.360
based on what they need.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.800
This is a bit of a
forward-looking picture.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:04.240
You'll see the large
Salesforce piece in the middle.

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:07.540
That is mainly because
that is our chosen CRM

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:09.370
here on campus.

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:12.070
How many of you are Salesforce users now?

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:14.220
Do we have, we've got
a, okay, there we go.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.180
So it's a really cool thing.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:21.750
So this isn't, this represents
where we're trying to get to.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:22.930
Once we start to knit together

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:24.310
all of these different technologies,

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:26.484
they feed into that Salesforce CRM,

00:01:26.484 --> 00:01:30.870
and that CRM is what's
gonna be able to generate

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:33.450
that 360-degree view of
what somebody's experience

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:35.090
is here on campus.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:37.160
It's not necessarily where we're at today.

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:41.270
Where we are at today
is we have Salesforce

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:42.880
as a CRM layer and it

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:46.750
consumes all of the data

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:50.320
from all of our upstream systems of record

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:51.910
and so everybody that we communicate with

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:56.460
in the Yale community gets
channeled into this CRM layer,

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:59.800
as well as anybody that is
interested in our content

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:02.450
around the globe gets
channeled into this CRM layer,

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:05.660
and then that layer feeds
Marketing Cloud or Yale Message,

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:07.740
which is our digital engagement platform,

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.696
which Mike van Emmenes is gonna talk

00:02:09.696 --> 00:02:11.196
to you about in just a minute.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.370
But there's also other
periphery technologies

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:18.270
that we offer here at Yale
that we're just starting now

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:22.120
to pull into and
integrate with Salesforce,

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:25.020
so Cvent, which is our conferencing.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:28.290
Cvent was a technology that
organized today's conference,

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:29.733
the Digital Conference.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:34.590
And Qualtrics is a survey tool,

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:38.440
also integrates in with Salesforce

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:39.810
as do the other technologies

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:42.400
that we're gonna talk to you about today.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:45.080
So this is our actual agenda for today,

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:47.450
so I'm gonna start off
talking to you a little bit

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:50.660
about what we do on the customer
relationship management,

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:55.090
and I recognize that we are
an institution, a university.

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:56.810
We do not have customers.

00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:58.240
Customer relationship management

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:00.900
is the industry's word for it,

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:02.780
but please feel free to substitute

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:07.623
that C for colleague or
constituent or community.

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:10.870
Then I'm gonna hand off
to Mike van Emmenes,

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:11.703
and he's gonna talk to you

00:03:11.703 --> 00:03:14.530
about digital engagement
using Yale Message,

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:16.730
and then James Tucciarone
is going to take us

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:19.740
the rest of the way and talk
to us about calendaring events,

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:22.613
room reservation systems,
and survey here on campus.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:28.453
So starting off with customer
relationship management.

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:32.120
This is really the heart
of the understanding

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:37.120
of what the community's
experience is that you serve.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:40.450
You noticed it on the other pages

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:42.890
being that center focal point.

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:45.590
It tracks all the
interactions in conjunction

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:46.900
with your business process,

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:49.420
so it doesn't have to be around people,

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:52.040
it can certainly, it's
commonly around people,

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:54.210
but it can also be between assets

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:58.930
like equipment located in rooms
or people attending events.

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:00.700
It's very flexible and it's flexible

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:02.573
to meet your business process.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:06.077
You might be thinking, it's
like, "Okay, but I track

00:04:06.077 --> 00:04:09.990
"interactions today anyway", and so do I.

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:11.580
You've got Outlook.

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:13.620
We hear a lot about Excel.

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:18.110
Outlook, hey, I also group
my emails and conversations

00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:19.160
so I can see who's talking

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:21.760
to when and what that
response part has been,

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:24.770
but when we talk about CRM,

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:27.080
we talk about taking those conversations

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:28.760
and then we put them in context

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:30.660
of what your business process is.

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:32.620
So if you have study for example,

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:35.700
and say you have a team
that's working on this study.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:38.513
You're having this email
conversation with somebody.

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:41.760
Is that at the beginning of that process?

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:43.180
Is it in the middle of that process?

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:44.480
Is it at the end of that process?

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:47.680
So it can put those
conversations into context

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:49.160
of what your business process is

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:51.563
and what you're trying to accomplish.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:55.530
The other thing with Outlook
is those are conversations

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:58.000
that are just between you and
somebody and might exclude

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.283
the rest of your team
that's working on it.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:03.430
With Outlook, it integrates
in with Salesforce,

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:07.540
you can actually just assign
those email conversations

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:10.750
directly into your CRM
and so it tracks it there

00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:12.630
and it gives it visibility
to the rest of the team

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:14.790
so we all are on the same page

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:16.650
walking through the business process.

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:18.493
It's very cool, very powerful,

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:22.390
but it's about adding that
context and that visibility

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:26.663
of where someone or something
is in their engagement path.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:30.290
We do hear a lot about
that Excel spreadsheet

00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:32.520
that different groups use
to track their interactions,

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:37.210
and this is just instead of it
being a very flat spreadsheet

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:39.480
that's hard to keep up with
this is a many-to-many type

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:42.990
of relationship that is
more robust and allows you

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:47.053
to actually track and have
visibility into your process.

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:49.343
It's all about process.

00:05:51.372 --> 00:05:53.260
It's very flexible.

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:55.800
We chose Salesforce many, many years ago

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:57.690
both tactically and strategically.

00:05:57.690 --> 00:05:59.480
If you think about Yale as an institution,

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:01.300
we support many different industries,

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:03.320
and many different schools and units

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:05.340
and we all have different
business processes,

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:07.550
so we needed something
that could be flexible

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:10.883
to adapt to each of those
different, unique processes,

00:06:12.410 --> 00:06:16.080
but still being centered
around whatever that object is

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:17.750
that you're trying to understand better,

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:19.900
whether it be a person
or an asset or something,

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:24.253
and Salesforce gives us that,
which is why it's on campus.

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:27.760
I'm gonna give you a
few different examples

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:32.130
of instances we have on campus,

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:35.750
so this was a really fun one
with the Psychology Department

00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:38.230
where they're using Salesforce in a way

00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:41.840
to understand families and
their children and the studies

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:44.900
that they are participating
in now and historically,

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:47.800
as well as showing things
like tracking appointments

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:49.363
and no-shows and shows.

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:55.470
Another example on campus
is Conferences and Events.

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:58.120
We'll call this a CRM plus.

00:06:58.120 --> 00:06:59.870
With them what we did is we configured it

00:06:59.870 --> 00:07:03.880
so that they can track
the end-to-end process,

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:06.040
so from the time somebody
reaches out as a lead

00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:09.050
to maybe hosting events on campus,

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:11.290
walking them all the
way through the process

00:07:11.290 --> 00:07:14.560
to the time that they're
actually managing the logistics

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:16.663
of that event on campus,

00:07:18.583 --> 00:07:21.500
and it also manages support
issues during the event

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:23.093
using a case management module.

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:33.220
And this one is also another
fun example of a CRM on campus.

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:35.630
It's for the Yale School of Public Health.

00:07:35.630 --> 00:07:38.300
If you can imagine Match.com,

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:41.510
where you have a lot of
students that need mentoring

00:07:41.510 --> 00:07:44.370
and alumni that might
want to act as mentors,

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:48.040
and so it takes alumni
opting in to be a mentor,

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:50.150
creating a profile for themselves,

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:52.330
and then allowing the students to come in

00:07:52.330 --> 00:07:55.790
and research the different
alumni that have opted in

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:00.850
and then they can favorite
them and then they can start

00:08:00.850 --> 00:08:04.513
building their own relationship
with their new mentor.

00:08:07.790 --> 00:08:10.520
It's very fun, very cool,
very powerful stuff,

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:12.260
and once you have all
these types of things

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:15.990
within your process documented
and you're working through it

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:17.860
you start to have some
really rich analytics

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:20.050
around that and once
you have those analytics

00:08:20.050 --> 00:08:22.780
it gives you even more
insight and more understanding

00:08:22.780 --> 00:08:26.470
about what the possibilities
could be to help

00:08:26.470 --> 00:08:28.170
or to meet your community's needs.

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:31.054
So you might be thinking
to yourself right now,

00:08:31.054 --> 00:08:32.377
it's like, "Gosh, I didn't
know all this stuff,

00:08:32.377 --> 00:08:35.140
"I would love to reach
out and understand more

00:08:35.140 --> 00:08:37.420
and see what else we can do".

00:08:37.420 --> 00:08:39.120
So what is it like to engage with us?

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:43.420
So for us, it's you just reach out

00:08:43.420 --> 00:08:44.960
and we go through some vetting

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:47.140
to make sure that we're
understanding which technology

00:08:47.140 --> 00:08:48.920
is the right choice for the problems

00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:50.390
that you're trying to have,

00:08:50.390 --> 00:08:52.140
and so let's say that it does happen

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:55.100
to be Salesforce on the CRM side.

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:58.470
What we would do is we'd
really get to engage with you

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:01.617
and really understand what
you're doing right now,

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:06.630
how you're doing it, and
how you wanna do it better,

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:07.750
and what your goals are,

00:09:07.750 --> 00:09:12.370
and so once we start to
document out that entire process

00:09:12.370 --> 00:09:14.650
we all get on the same page with that

00:09:14.650 --> 00:09:16.970
and then we configure the tool for you

00:09:16.970 --> 00:09:18.710
and it's a whole lot easier to adopt

00:09:18.710 --> 00:09:21.560
and we've seen a lot of success
with doing that approach.

00:09:22.550 --> 00:09:23.630
So that's my time,

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:25.630
and I'm gonna hand this over now to Mike

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:27.583
to talk to you about Yale Message.

00:09:29.378 --> 00:09:30.440
(audience clapping)

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:31.378
Thank you.

00:09:31.378 --> 00:09:32.211
Thank you.

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:36.390
- Good afternoon everyone.

00:09:36.390 --> 00:09:38.063
Who uses Yale Message here?

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:40.080
Majority of the room.

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:42.313
Who attended our session
just before lunch?

00:09:43.165 --> 00:09:45.360
(Mike laughing)

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:47.680
So some of this might be
regurgitation for those people,

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:49.400
for other people, welcome.

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:53.667
Yale Message is a digital
communications platform

00:09:55.890 --> 00:09:57.163
built on Salesforce.

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:02.960
In our current state we are
focusing on two key features.

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:06.713
We're focusing on the Email
Studio and Journey Builder.

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:09.920
Email Studio is precisely what that is,

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:12.960
it's sending email
communications to the community.

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:16.050
We gather data from our
data sources here at Yale,

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:19.633
so we're connected into the
People Hub, WorkDay, Hopper.

00:10:21.305 --> 00:10:23.830
And those lists can be updated

00:10:23.830 --> 00:10:25.803
as those systems of record are updated.

00:10:27.020 --> 00:10:28.080
The other portion that we are

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:30.580
currently dealing with is Journey Builder.

00:10:30.580 --> 00:10:34.110
Journey Builder's a really
cool feature in that you build

00:10:34.110 --> 00:10:36.500
a journey for your list of people

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:37.900
who you're communicating with,

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:40.340
so you would have the list of people,

00:10:40.340 --> 00:10:43.700
you would set up the
journey to go as expected,

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:45.010
send out a message,

00:10:45.010 --> 00:10:46.960
and dependent on what each individual does

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:48.120
within that journey,

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:50.463
will send them down a
different type of path.

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:53.420
We've got a really cool
journey set up right now

00:10:53.420 --> 00:10:55.020
with organizational development.

00:10:56.350 --> 00:10:57.920
Travis is doing a phenomenal job

00:10:57.920 --> 00:10:59.877
so we appreciate the
partnership, thank you.

00:10:59.877 --> 00:11:02.877
(audience clapping)

00:11:04.226 --> 00:11:05.059
(audience laughing)

00:11:05.059 --> 00:11:07.600
But something that Travis
does is that there are

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:11.130
certain classes that are set
up and participants need to do

00:11:11.130 --> 00:11:12.700
required reading before the class,

00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:14.990
so the individuals on the list

00:11:14.990 --> 00:11:18.580
will get the email through the journey.

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:20.930
If that person, if someone
does not open the email,

00:11:20.930 --> 00:11:24.020
or if someone does not click
on the link within that email,

00:11:24.020 --> 00:11:26.180
a different journey will be started up.

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:29.770
So if I'm in a class and my
class starts next Monday,

00:11:29.770 --> 00:11:31.590
the journey started today, I got my email,

00:11:31.590 --> 00:11:33.300
I just kept ignoring it,

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:36.430
Travis has set up that within
two days of me receiving it,

00:11:36.430 --> 00:11:37.450
ping Michael again.

00:11:37.450 --> 00:11:40.030
Ping him, ping him
until he opens the email

00:11:40.030 --> 00:11:42.430
or until he clicks on the link

00:11:42.430 --> 00:11:44.720
so that I read the required reading.

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:47.070
If I don't do either of those
and I show up for my class

00:11:47.070 --> 00:11:48.800
and I'm ready to rock and roll,

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:51.350
the instructor knows that
I haven't done anything

00:11:51.350 --> 00:11:54.157
and could possibly say, "Goodbye Michael,

00:11:54.157 --> 00:11:56.967
"come back next time when you've
done the prescribed reading

00:11:56.967 --> 00:11:59.117
"that we need for you
to do for the class."

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:03.810
In the future, we've got
two other great tools

00:12:03.810 --> 00:12:05.280
that we're gonna focus on.

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:08.130
Mobile Studio, which is mobile messaging,

00:12:08.130 --> 00:12:10.680
which can also be integrated
with the Journey Builder.

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:14.030
So Travis is super excited about that.

00:12:14.030 --> 00:12:16.670
We know today's day and age
everyone likes their cellphone,

00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:19.590
so if you don't read the
reading or open the email

00:12:19.590 --> 00:12:21.190
we can then create a journey

00:12:21.190 --> 00:12:24.360
that starts sending text
messages to your phone

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:26.440
if you've opted in for that.

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:29.810
And then Social Studio.

00:12:29.810 --> 00:12:32.430
We all know the power of social media.

00:12:32.430 --> 00:12:33.363
It's coming.

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:35.420
We're gonna get there.

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:41.740
Just some stats on where your
Yale Message is on the campus.

00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:43.330
First off, it's a kudos to the team.

00:12:43.330 --> 00:12:45.610
Today or June is our one-year anniversary

00:12:45.610 --> 00:12:47.600
since we adopted Yale Message,

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:49.670
and in that time frame we've brought on

00:12:49.670 --> 00:12:53.750
over 200 departments,
trained over 750 people,

00:12:53.750 --> 00:12:57.973
we've got about 32 other
departments ready to come on board.

00:13:00.570 --> 00:13:03.730
Another great success is up here.

00:13:03.730 --> 00:13:05.900
We are with the Yale brand,
we've got the Yale font,

00:13:05.900 --> 00:13:07.700
and we are freely accessible,

00:13:07.700 --> 00:13:10.083
which we know is a huge requisite.

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:15.210
Another great statistic, year-to-date,

00:13:15.210 --> 00:13:17.230
so in the first five months of this year,

00:13:17.230 --> 00:13:20.210
we have successfully
sent over 30,000 emails

00:13:20.210 --> 00:13:23.710
to 34 million recipients.

00:13:23.710 --> 00:13:26.770
We've had an open success
rate of about 40%,

00:13:26.770 --> 00:13:29.520
which is roughly about
8% better than it was

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:30.670
with our previous tool.

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:38.280
The other great feature with Yale Message

00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:39.640
is obviously the analytics.

00:13:39.640 --> 00:13:42.150
As Dan was referring to, pulling the data,

00:13:42.150 --> 00:13:43.423
understanding the data.

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:46.630
With analytics that we receive,

00:13:46.630 --> 00:13:50.730
we're able to identify who
the targeted audience truly is

00:13:50.730 --> 00:13:52.370
and how they've interacted with us,

00:13:52.370 --> 00:13:56.190
so if you send out an email
to, let's do my other number,

00:13:56.190 --> 00:13:58.800
34 million people, which, pretty huge,

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:01.330
but only a million people
start interacting with you,

00:14:01.330 --> 00:14:03.240
you've now got your targeted audience.

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:06.363
You know who they are, and
you target them specifically.

00:14:07.490 --> 00:14:12.130
We are also able to get
open rates, click rates.

00:14:12.130 --> 00:14:13.870
So you'll know who's opening your emails,

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:15.800
we'll know who's clicking on their emails.

00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:18.490
There's a report within the email

00:14:18.490 --> 00:14:21.280
of which clicks receive the
highest percentage of clicks.

00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:24.943
So what your audience is actually
reading within that email.

00:14:26.450 --> 00:14:28.513
Over here you can see a Delivery Rate.

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:32.423
We strive for that 99%
to 100% Delivery Rate.

00:14:35.160 --> 00:14:39.210
You find out why people are
not receiving the emails.

00:14:39.210 --> 00:14:41.020
If it's a hard bounce,

00:14:41.020 --> 00:14:43.690
you can click on the
hyperlink and you can see

00:14:43.690 --> 00:14:46.010
if that email address
was incorrectly written

00:14:46.010 --> 00:14:48.060
or that email address just doesn't exist.

00:14:49.390 --> 00:14:53.500
Soft bounce, if a
person's mailbox is full,

00:14:53.500 --> 00:14:55.150
Yale Message will continue to try send

00:14:55.150 --> 00:14:57.770
that email to the person for 72 hours,

00:14:57.770 --> 00:14:59.680
hopefully getting through to them,

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:01.700
and then obviously a block bounce,

00:15:01.700 --> 00:15:03.340
unfortunately there's not much we can do

00:15:03.340 --> 00:15:04.173
with that in the real world.

00:15:04.173 --> 00:15:07.420
It's someone's gone out and
specifically blocked you.

00:15:07.420 --> 00:15:10.250
We did work with Salesforce
and we keep up with Salesforce

00:15:10.250 --> 00:15:13.300
on white-listing our domain name.

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:16.333
Right now our domain is message.yale.edu.

00:15:17.490 --> 00:15:20.780
It is at a higher white
list than yale.edu.

00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:23.153
We're able to track it,
we're able to push it,

00:15:24.140 --> 00:15:26.320
and we work with different
constituents to make sure

00:15:26.320 --> 00:15:28.193
that emails get pushed through.

00:15:29.290 --> 00:15:31.310
And then some really cool
features is when you start

00:15:31.310 --> 00:15:33.580
to get into your email engagement,

00:15:33.580 --> 00:15:37.770
you can see percentages of
who's gonna click on emails,

00:15:37.770 --> 00:15:41.090
who's gonna open emails,
so you get a true sense

00:15:41.090 --> 00:15:43.923
of how your community's engaging with you.

00:15:48.344 --> 00:15:49.370
And that's it.

00:15:49.370 --> 00:15:51.393
Up next, I'm gonna hand over to James.

00:15:52.882 --> 00:15:55.889
(audience clapping)

00:15:55.889 --> 00:15:56.722
Thank you.

00:16:00.800 --> 00:16:01.633
- All right.

00:16:01.633 --> 00:16:04.270
So, I'll be taking over to talk to you all

00:16:04.270 --> 00:16:07.170
about our calendaring and
event management tools.

00:16:07.170 --> 00:16:08.960
Also our room reservation system

00:16:08.960 --> 00:16:11.060
and our survey tool that we use on campus.

00:16:11.060 --> 00:16:13.360
So to get started, we're gonna take a look

00:16:13.360 --> 00:16:14.680
at calendaring and event management

00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:18.500
which we support through
Bedework, Cvent and CrowdCompass.

00:16:18.500 --> 00:16:20.370
Basically what we're trying
to do here is provide

00:16:20.370 --> 00:16:22.100
a common set of tools that will help

00:16:22.100 --> 00:16:24.600
in understanding your
target audience's engagement

00:16:26.280 --> 00:16:28.960
as well as to facilitate
some policy adherence

00:16:28.960 --> 00:16:31.100
while meeting some of
our critical requirements

00:16:31.100 --> 00:16:32.700
in this field around campus.

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:34.760
So some of those
requirements are going to be

00:16:34.760 --> 00:16:37.350
calendar publishing and cross listing,

00:16:37.350 --> 00:16:38.810
submission and approval.

00:16:38.810 --> 00:16:40.980
Also third-party integration.

00:16:40.980 --> 00:16:43.120
We're looking to do event
and session management

00:16:43.120 --> 00:16:44.830
as well as payment processing,

00:16:44.830 --> 00:16:47.770
and then a mobile attendee
experience which is really neat,

00:16:47.770 --> 00:16:50.030
and of course, as we've
mentioned a few times already,

00:16:50.030 --> 00:16:51.633
reporting on top of all of this.

00:16:52.470 --> 00:16:54.190
So I'm gonna break down the difference

00:16:54.190 --> 00:16:55.980
between calendaring and
event management just a bit

00:16:55.980 --> 00:16:57.980
because we've found over the years

00:16:57.980 --> 00:17:00.020
that there is sometimes
a bit of a difference

00:17:00.020 --> 00:17:02.250
in what people define as calendaring

00:17:02.250 --> 00:17:03.680
and also events management.

00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:04.800
So looking at calendaring,

00:17:04.800 --> 00:17:07.020
here we have our Yale Calendar of Events.

00:17:07.020 --> 00:17:08.780
This is also know as the Bedework system,

00:17:08.780 --> 00:17:10.600
it's at calendar.yale.edu,

00:17:10.600 --> 00:17:13.120
and basically this is
our unified event hub

00:17:13.120 --> 00:17:14.093
for the university.

00:17:15.460 --> 00:17:18.530
It's a great opportunity
for us to really be a part

00:17:18.530 --> 00:17:21.650
of the vision of a unified
Yale here on campus,

00:17:21.650 --> 00:17:24.210
and it's intended to
be the one place to go

00:17:24.210 --> 00:17:26.730
for all of the events that
are taking place on campus,

00:17:26.730 --> 00:17:29.960
and in that way, it improves
the experience for people

00:17:29.960 --> 00:17:31.940
that are looking to
attend events on campus

00:17:31.940 --> 00:17:33.820
as well as the general Yale community

00:17:33.820 --> 00:17:35.670
and also some departments around campus

00:17:35.670 --> 00:17:38.470
such as Public, excuse me,
Public Safety and Facilities.

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:42.680
So it also improves
visibility of your events

00:17:42.680 --> 00:17:46.180
as calendar.yale.edu is calendar.yale.edu

00:17:46.180 --> 00:17:48.970
and it's linked directly from yale.edu,

00:17:48.970 --> 00:17:50.660
so it really pushes people to come

00:17:50.660 --> 00:17:54.260
right to this calendar
system and really market

00:17:54.260 --> 00:17:56.090
what is taking place here around campus.

00:17:56.090 --> 00:17:58.170
So with the calendar here,

00:17:58.170 --> 00:17:59.640
the requirements that we're trying to meet

00:17:59.640 --> 00:18:02.200
are going to be those calendar
publishing and cross listing,

00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:04.340
as well as the submission and approval,

00:18:04.340 --> 00:18:06.820
and then of course we have
third-party integrations

00:18:06.820 --> 00:18:08.650
which are really interesting, both custom,

00:18:08.650 --> 00:18:10.930
such as our integration
with the Yale Arts Calendar,

00:18:10.930 --> 00:18:13.210
and also out-of-the-box
such as our integration

00:18:13.210 --> 00:18:14.203
with Yale sites.

00:18:16.270 --> 00:18:18.970
And actually I'll pop back
there for just a moment.

00:18:18.970 --> 00:18:20.440
So in here on the left-hand side,

00:18:20.440 --> 00:18:24.140
we can see our landing
page for calendar.yale.edu,

00:18:24.140 --> 00:18:26.040
and one thing to note about this is

00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:28.390
that the Office of Public
Affairs and Communications

00:18:28.390 --> 00:18:30.910
is our functional owner
for the calendar here,

00:18:30.910 --> 00:18:33.930
however the individual
groups who are posting events

00:18:33.930 --> 00:18:36.450
retain administrative
ownership of their events,

00:18:36.450 --> 00:18:38.940
and some of the other groups
that are using the calendar

00:18:38.940 --> 00:18:41.610
include the Graduate School
of Arts and Sciences,

00:18:41.610 --> 00:18:44.760
the MacMillan Center programs,
the School of Management,

00:18:44.760 --> 00:18:45.650
we have West Campus,

00:18:45.650 --> 00:18:47.210
we have approximately 100 different groups

00:18:47.210 --> 00:18:49.413
that are actually using
the calendar currently.

00:18:50.330 --> 00:18:53.510
And so now moving into our
event management tools,

00:18:53.510 --> 00:18:56.090
which we support with
Cvent here on campus,

00:18:56.090 --> 00:18:58.990
and Cvent is an event
management system that automates

00:18:58.990 --> 00:19:01.610
and simplifies the entire
event planning process,

00:19:01.610 --> 00:19:04.330
and it offers tools that
run the entire gamut

00:19:04.330 --> 00:19:07.000
from vendor, excuse me, venue sourcing,

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:08.950
all the way to real-time dashboards,

00:19:08.950 --> 00:19:11.350
and reporting at the end of your event.

00:19:11.350 --> 00:19:13.640
So we can see here it provides us

00:19:13.640 --> 00:19:15.910
with a number of different opportunities.

00:19:15.910 --> 00:19:16.870
In our lens here,

00:19:16.870 --> 00:19:18.550
some of the requirements we can see

00:19:18.550 --> 00:19:20.320
that we're meeting are
gonna be event registration

00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:21.740
and payment processing.

00:19:21.740 --> 00:19:23.430
Of course we also have marketing,

00:19:23.430 --> 00:19:24.660
which is great and fits right in

00:19:24.660 --> 00:19:26.010
with some of our other tools,

00:19:26.010 --> 00:19:28.200
and then some of our add-ons
which are really neat

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:29.830
is we have an on-site solution

00:19:29.830 --> 00:19:31.870
which Cvent calls their
white glove service,

00:19:31.870 --> 00:19:35.560
where they send people down
here to actually man your event

00:19:35.560 --> 00:19:37.370
and they handle the registration process

00:19:37.370 --> 00:19:39.930
where they will print ID
cards, handle check-in,

00:19:39.930 --> 00:19:41.690
get everybody set up, et cetera,

00:19:41.690 --> 00:19:42.910
and something else that they offer

00:19:42.910 --> 00:19:44.760
which is really cool is live polling.

00:19:44.760 --> 00:19:46.670
So during a presentation such as this,

00:19:46.670 --> 00:19:48.670
you can actually get
feedback from your audience

00:19:48.670 --> 00:19:50.780
and have an interactive
discussion about something

00:19:50.780 --> 00:19:52.600
that you're actually talking about.

00:19:52.600 --> 00:19:54.620
So as Dan mentioned earlier,

00:19:54.620 --> 00:19:56.760
everybody here today had
an experience with Cvent

00:19:56.760 --> 00:19:59.090
when they registered for the
Digital Conference, right?

00:19:59.090 --> 00:20:00.470
And then a number of other groups

00:20:00.470 --> 00:20:02.180
around campus are using it as well,

00:20:02.180 --> 00:20:05.190
so the Office of Development
and the Yale Alumni Association

00:20:05.190 --> 00:20:08.060
are two large users, as
well as the Law School,

00:20:08.060 --> 00:20:09.400
School of Management.

00:20:09.400 --> 00:20:12.610
We also have Athletics
which is really interesting.

00:20:12.610 --> 00:20:14.680
And then just a couple of the features

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:16.000
that are in here with Cvent,

00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:18.250
as we saw already with this lens here.

00:20:18.250 --> 00:20:21.350
So we have an online
event registration program

00:20:21.350 --> 00:20:24.290
which provides also a
branded website experience,

00:20:24.290 --> 00:20:25.480
and then of course provides us

00:20:25.480 --> 00:20:27.290
that secure payment processing.

00:20:27.290 --> 00:20:29.610
The tool also allows us
to manage our attendees

00:20:29.610 --> 00:20:33.810
as well as our speakers and our
exhibitors and our sessions,

00:20:33.810 --> 00:20:35.630
and then of course you know it provides us

00:20:35.630 --> 00:20:37.720
with that robust reporting mechanism

00:20:37.720 --> 00:20:40.120
that is really required
across all of our tools.

00:20:41.430 --> 00:20:44.730
So that steps us into our
mobile attendee engagement

00:20:44.730 --> 00:20:47.460
or experience application,
and this is CrowdCompass,

00:20:47.460 --> 00:20:48.970
which is a subsidiary of Cvent,

00:20:48.970 --> 00:20:51.890
and basically it's a mobile
application that your users

00:20:51.890 --> 00:20:54.340
can use while they're
actually attending an event,

00:20:54.340 --> 00:20:57.560
and basically, what Cvent, excuse me,

00:20:57.560 --> 00:21:00.670
what CrowdCompass states is
that there's two primary reasons

00:21:00.670 --> 00:21:02.490
that people attend a
conference or an event,

00:21:02.490 --> 00:21:04.600
and that's so that they can
engage with your content,

00:21:04.600 --> 00:21:06.520
and so that they can
engage with other people,

00:21:06.520 --> 00:21:09.000
and CrowdCompass is
really, really powerful

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:11.760
for doing that as it allows
us to engage with our peers

00:21:11.760 --> 00:21:14.250
through social networking and
it allows us to get engaged

00:21:14.250 --> 00:21:17.320
with our content and our
speakers through attendee

00:21:17.320 --> 00:21:19.590
and speaker tools that are available.

00:21:19.590 --> 00:21:21.550
The other thing that it
provides is an ability

00:21:21.550 --> 00:21:23.270
for us to have another opportunity

00:21:23.270 --> 00:21:26.663
to engage our attendees
with our brand in general.

00:21:27.770 --> 00:21:30.800
So we can see some of the,
in our second bullet here,

00:21:30.800 --> 00:21:32.760
some of the items that are really useful

00:21:32.760 --> 00:21:35.690
within the system is going
to be the session scheduling,

00:21:35.690 --> 00:21:38.150
we have speaker profiling,
interactive maps,

00:21:38.150 --> 00:21:39.610
and push notifications.

00:21:39.610 --> 00:21:41.300
A couple of really interesting things

00:21:41.300 --> 00:21:43.620
that have been coming about
lately is we've been seeing

00:21:43.620 --> 00:21:46.730
a lot of integration with
augmented reality and wayfinding.

00:21:46.730 --> 00:21:50.540
So for instance, at a Facebook
conference a few years back,

00:21:50.540 --> 00:21:53.570
they actually had one of
these nice little badges here

00:21:53.570 --> 00:21:55.450
where you flipped it over,
looked at it with your phone

00:21:55.450 --> 00:21:58.990
and you had a 3D floor plan
representation of the campus

00:21:58.990 --> 00:22:01.180
that the Facebook conference
was taking place at.

00:22:01.180 --> 00:22:03.190
Another example we've
seen is using your phone

00:22:03.190 --> 00:22:06.530
to look where you're going
and having a dotted line

00:22:06.530 --> 00:22:08.770
that's wayfinding you to
wherever your location is.

00:22:08.770 --> 00:22:10.270
So for somebody that's really not familiar

00:22:10.270 --> 00:22:12.740
with the campus or the location,
it's a great way, I mean,

00:22:12.740 --> 00:22:14.570
hopefully we still watch
and check both ways

00:22:14.570 --> 00:22:17.660
before we turn the street,
or cross the street rather,

00:22:17.660 --> 00:22:20.190
but one really, really interesting thing

00:22:20.190 --> 00:22:22.750
that I find is that we see the statistic

00:22:22.750 --> 00:22:25.450
that on average attendees for a conference

00:22:25.450 --> 00:22:27.750
check their phone 200 times a day.

00:22:27.750 --> 00:22:29.290
So if we consider that statistic,

00:22:29.290 --> 00:22:32.110
we can see how very easily CrowdCompass

00:22:32.110 --> 00:22:34.113
can become such a powerful tool.

00:22:35.880 --> 00:22:37.760
And of course with CrowdCompass,

00:22:37.760 --> 00:22:40.070
some of the groups that are
using this is Commencement,

00:22:40.070 --> 00:22:42.910
one of our biggest events that
are taking place on campus.

00:22:42.910 --> 00:22:45.060
We also do the Yale-Harvard weekend.

00:22:45.060 --> 00:22:47.270
We have the School of
Management Welcome Weekend,

00:22:47.270 --> 00:22:48.840
we have some orientations,

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:51.960
so we have a number of
sort of high profile events

00:22:51.960 --> 00:22:53.980
that are being used
through this application,

00:22:53.980 --> 00:22:55.730
and again, the great thing about this is

00:22:55.730 --> 00:22:58.880
it really gets right to
the heart of your attendees

00:22:58.880 --> 00:23:00.100
and it gives them an experience

00:23:00.100 --> 00:23:01.580
that's beyond just registration

00:23:01.580 --> 00:23:03.480
and understanding what's taking place.

00:23:04.990 --> 00:23:07.450
So moving on now, I'm
gonna talk about our room

00:23:07.450 --> 00:23:08.810
and booking management system.

00:23:08.810 --> 00:23:10.970
So this is the room reservation system.

00:23:10.970 --> 00:23:13.130
Some of your might also know this as EMS,

00:23:13.130 --> 00:23:14.700
which is Event Management System,

00:23:14.700 --> 00:23:18.090
as opposed to Expense or
Emergency Management System.

00:23:18.090 --> 00:23:20.460
There's no confusion
with that at all, right?

00:23:20.460 --> 00:23:23.420
So, basically, EMS is our space

00:23:23.420 --> 00:23:25.210
and booking management system.

00:23:25.210 --> 00:23:27.910
It also allows for some resource booking.

00:23:27.910 --> 00:23:29.720
One of the great things
about this is it allows

00:23:29.720 --> 00:23:32.260
for granular access and when
appropriate we can also do

00:23:32.260 --> 00:23:34.130
an automated user load.

00:23:34.130 --> 00:23:35.870
There's also an, excuse me,

00:23:35.870 --> 00:23:38.650
an integration with Exchange
as well as with Banner,

00:23:38.650 --> 00:23:40.740
and it's really powerful in its ability

00:23:40.740 --> 00:23:42.770
to provide us with some functionality

00:23:42.770 --> 00:23:45.630
around scheduling
scenarios and optimization,

00:23:45.630 --> 00:23:48.890
and we also have of course API access

00:23:51.192 --> 00:23:53.210
and let's see here, and then, of course,

00:23:53.210 --> 00:23:54.760
as with all of our other tools,

00:23:54.760 --> 00:23:57.540
have a robust reporting mechanism as well.

00:23:57.540 --> 00:23:59.050
So we can see that there's a number

00:23:59.050 --> 00:24:00.970
of different things that EMS can do,

00:24:00.970 --> 00:24:04.640
and I like to call it a space
and object management system

00:24:04.640 --> 00:24:06.680
because it's not really
just about rooms anymore.

00:24:06.680 --> 00:24:09.270
In fact, the School of
Drama is using this system

00:24:09.270 --> 00:24:11.010
to manage some of their
vehicles that they allow

00:24:11.010 --> 00:24:12.830
for check-in and check-out.

00:24:12.830 --> 00:24:15.510
And in addition to the School of Drama,

00:24:15.510 --> 00:24:16.860
we also have the Law School,

00:24:16.860 --> 00:24:18.740
which is actually the
group that implemented EMS

00:24:18.740 --> 00:24:20.300
on campus about 10 years ago,

00:24:20.300 --> 00:24:22.970
and then the School of Public
Health sort of adopted it

00:24:22.970 --> 00:24:25.290
as well before it became
centrally managed,

00:24:25.290 --> 00:24:28.280
and now we have about two
dozen different groups,

00:24:28.280 --> 00:24:29.550
including School of Management,

00:24:29.550 --> 00:24:33.630
who in all fairness is managing
their own instance of EMS,

00:24:33.630 --> 00:24:35.320
however they are using EMS,

00:24:35.320 --> 00:24:38.100
and then we also are recently rolling out

00:24:38.100 --> 00:24:41.680
the Medical Library,
YCBA, the Art Gallery,

00:24:41.680 --> 00:24:45.550
and a number of various
departments around campus.

00:24:45.550 --> 00:24:49.880
So one of the most exciting things for me

00:24:49.880 --> 00:24:52.830
that are coming about right
now is digital room signage.

00:24:52.830 --> 00:24:56.130
So digital room signage
is a tablet-sized device

00:24:56.130 --> 00:24:58.540
that is installed outside of a space,

00:24:58.540 --> 00:25:00.470
and basically it provides an application

00:25:00.470 --> 00:25:02.640
that shows you whether or
not the room is available

00:25:02.640 --> 00:25:04.070
and what's taking place in that room

00:25:04.070 --> 00:25:05.500
over the course of the day.

00:25:05.500 --> 00:25:06.860
Also, if you choose to do so,

00:25:06.860 --> 00:25:08.560
you can have people
interact with this device

00:25:08.560 --> 00:25:09.980
as they're walking up to a space.

00:25:09.980 --> 00:25:11.230
So if the space is available,

00:25:11.230 --> 00:25:13.300
somebody can walk right
up and reserve the space,

00:25:13.300 --> 00:25:15.040
or if you have a lot
of problems with people

00:25:15.040 --> 00:25:16.690
not actually showing up
for their reservations

00:25:16.690 --> 00:25:18.540
you can actually require them to check in

00:25:18.540 --> 00:25:20.400
so that that way if they
don't check in the space

00:25:20.400 --> 00:25:22.500
is released and somebody
else can then use that space

00:25:22.500 --> 00:25:25.150
and you're not just sitting
there with a wasted room.

00:25:26.070 --> 00:25:28.660
So this is an example of the application,

00:25:28.660 --> 00:25:31.480
and right now we have this
also implemented in a number

00:25:31.480 --> 00:25:32.780
of places around campus.

00:25:32.780 --> 00:25:35.700
The School of Management is
one really great example,

00:25:35.700 --> 00:25:37.150
and if you ever have an opportunity

00:25:37.150 --> 00:25:38.580
to go over to their site,

00:25:38.580 --> 00:25:40.660
you can see this on most
if not all of their rooms

00:25:40.660 --> 00:25:42.080
within the building.

00:25:42.080 --> 00:25:44.470
Divinity has also implemented
a number of these devices

00:25:44.470 --> 00:25:47.330
along with the Institute of Sacred Music.

00:25:47.330 --> 00:25:49.640
Nursing is going to be
putting in about 80 or so

00:25:49.640 --> 00:25:53.240
as a part of their renovation
process, and the Center,

00:25:53.240 --> 00:25:57.070
excuse me, the Cushing/Whitney
Medical Library

00:25:57.070 --> 00:25:59.180
has also implemented a
number of these as part

00:25:59.180 --> 00:26:01.130
of their renovation which
is actually, I believe,

00:26:01.130 --> 00:26:03.750
opening on Thursday, so
if anybody's interested

00:26:03.750 --> 00:26:05.200
in seeing them there as well.

00:26:06.190 --> 00:26:07.460
And here I just have an image.

00:26:07.460 --> 00:26:09.180
We can see a couple of, and I'm sorry,

00:26:09.180 --> 00:26:11.360
I know this is probably not
the most clearest image,

00:26:11.360 --> 00:26:12.980
but we can see that we have a couple

00:26:12.980 --> 00:26:15.210
of the devices here at SOM.

00:26:15.210 --> 00:26:17.940
We have one right over here and
we have one right over here,

00:26:17.940 --> 00:26:20.880
and with these particular
pieces of hardware,

00:26:20.880 --> 00:26:22.780
you can actually see
that there's an LED strip

00:26:22.780 --> 00:26:24.270
on either side of the device,

00:26:24.270 --> 00:26:26.490
so even if you don't walk
right up to the device,

00:26:26.490 --> 00:26:28.010
from a distance you can actually see

00:26:28.010 --> 00:26:29.420
whether or not a room is available

00:26:29.420 --> 00:26:31.360
and decide if you wanna go
ahead and go to that room

00:26:31.360 --> 00:26:32.943
and maybe have a meeting.

00:26:35.170 --> 00:26:36.950
And so that's gonna then bring us

00:26:36.950 --> 00:26:39.050
into survey management and design.

00:26:39.050 --> 00:26:41.990
So for that, we use Qualtrics
to support this need,

00:26:41.990 --> 00:26:45.190
and Qualtrics can do
so many things, right?

00:26:45.190 --> 00:26:47.290
And we can see just some
of those things right here.

00:26:47.290 --> 00:26:48.630
So we have student engagement,

00:26:48.630 --> 00:26:51.540
we have online course
evaluations, we have dashboards,

00:26:51.540 --> 00:26:54.000
we have patient care, we
have employee experience,

00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:55.850
and one of my favorite
things since this is

00:26:55.850 --> 00:26:58.900
sort of my area here is that
you can actually even use it

00:26:58.900 --> 00:27:02.460
for really simple registration
and RSVP, that's pretty cool.

00:27:02.460 --> 00:27:05.710
And so it's available to
all Yale faculty, staff,

00:27:05.710 --> 00:27:08.270
and students for the
business of the university,

00:27:08.270 --> 00:27:12.030
and in addition it is
approved for PHI data,

00:27:12.030 --> 00:27:14.830
and Qualtrics states that it is helpful

00:27:14.830 --> 00:27:17.730
in increasing the speed and
the quality of our research

00:27:17.730 --> 00:27:20.460
and overall it improves our
engagement, our experience,

00:27:20.460 --> 00:27:22.123
and our user satisfaction.

00:27:24.340 --> 00:27:27.410
And I think that's pretty much it for me,

00:27:27.410 --> 00:27:29.950
so I think I'll go ahead and
turn this back over to Dan

00:27:29.950 --> 00:27:31.290
for him to wrap us up,
(Dan laughing)

00:27:31.290 --> 00:27:32.840
and I thank you very much.

00:27:32.840 --> 00:27:34.580
- Yeah, thanks.
(audience clapping)

00:27:34.580 --> 00:27:35.413
Awesome.

00:27:38.258 --> 00:27:40.645
I think I just get the
final thank you slides.

00:27:40.645 --> 00:27:41.700
(audience member laughing)

00:27:41.700 --> 00:27:44.600
So just wrapping this back to
our forward-looking statement.

00:27:44.600 --> 00:27:45.900
So all the technologies that you saw,

00:27:45.900 --> 00:27:48.500
we are trying to actively
knit those together

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:52.080
into a Salesforce CRM so that we can start

00:27:52.080 --> 00:27:54.070
understanding the audience insights

00:27:54.070 --> 00:27:56.580
that we were talking about earlier,

00:27:56.580 --> 00:27:58.540
which is really, really awesome

00:27:58.540 --> 00:27:59.920
because then we can start to understand,

00:27:59.920 --> 00:28:03.020
it's like that one person
that comes into our community

00:28:03.020 --> 00:28:05.520
and how many different places
that they actually touch

00:28:05.520 --> 00:28:08.690
and how many departments and
understand what their interests

00:28:08.690 --> 00:28:11.623
actually are so that we
can better serve them.

00:28:12.553 --> 00:28:13.950
And with that, that's it.

00:28:13.950 --> 00:28:14.995
Thank you.

00:28:14.995 --> 00:28:17.985
